  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Northeast winds Friday night through Saturday will create\n   choppy conditions on area lakes.\n\n-  A cutoff low pressure system brings widespread rain, with snow\n   above 6,000 feet Saturday night through Monday morning.\n\nA fast-moving trough will drop south out of Canada today, bringing\nupper-level energy with it this evening and overnight. This will push a\nband of showers and isolated thunderstorms through western Montana and\nnorth-central Idaho by early Friday morning. Expect breezy westerly winds\ngusting 15 to 25 mph, along with brief, light to moderate rainfall of 0.10\nto 0.40 inches by sunrise Friday morning.\n\nBehind this trough, a cold front will move south out of Alberta, Canada,\nbringing breezy east to northeast winds Friday evening. These winds will\nremain over the area through the day on Saturday. Temperatures Friday and\nSaturday will run 5 to 10 degrees below normal for this time of year. It\nwill feel even cooler Saturday afternoon with east winds gusting around 20\nmph. The east winds will increase after sunset Saturday evening, with gusts\nup to 30 mph in the favored east-west gaps and canyons.\n\nWe continue to track a cutoff low pressure system that is forecast to move\nonshore of the Pacific Northwest on Saturday, passing over the Northern\nRockies Saturday evening and Sunday. This system will bring widespread rain\nand high mountain snow and much cooler temperatures that will make it feel\nmore like fall than late summer. The challenging part of the forecast has\nbeen the exact track of the low, which will play a huge role in where the\nheaviest rain and snow will fall. The track has been trending slightly\nnorth the past 24 hours, with the center of the low now moving over\nnorthwest Montana. In this scenario, the most widespread and persistent\nrain will be north of I-90, bringing snow levels down to around 6,000 feet\nin Glacier National Park and along the Continental Divide. Wherever the\ntrack of the low ends up being, wet, cool, and breezy conditions will be\npresent throughout the Northern Rockies, increasing the hypothermia risk in\nthe backcountry of western Montana and north-central Idaho. As it stands\nnow, we expect anywhere from 0.50 to 1.25 inches of rain to fall across\nmost of the region through Monday morning, with southwest Montana being on\nthe drier end of the system, where only about 0.25 inches are expected.\nThere is a 10 to 20 percent probability this northerly trend in the track\nwill continue. In that case, rain and snow amounts could be considerably\nless. Stay tuned.\n\nTemperatures will begin to moderate by mid-week next week, with afternoon\ntemperatures warming back closer to normal for this time of year, along\nwith a mix of clouds and sunshine.",
